  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/0/01/X-Men_Vol_1_3.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "While practicing, Professor X calls Cyclops \"Scott\" (the first time - he was referred to previously only as \"Slim\") and says he's probably the most powerful X-Man but also so grim.&nbsp; Scott worries about his power beam.&nbsp; Beast, Iceman and Angel clown around before the professor calls for Jean's turn at practice.&nbsp; But it is interrupted when the professor senses another mutant and sends the team off to investigate.&nbsp; They all fight over who gets to team up with Jean, of course, except Scott, who is too tormented by the possibility that someone might get hurt as a result of his power beams.\n\nIt is at this point (last panel of page 4) that we find out the Professor thinks of Jean as \"the one I love,\" but <i>yeesh</i>, I know this moment was ill-advised and thankfully is forgotten about for years.&nbsp; Jeans wants to go with Scott, but Warren scoops her up and drops her into his car and heads out!&nbsp; Iceman admits that Angel is the \"glamor boy\" of the group.&nbsp; Hank and Bobby are very vocal about their interest in Jean, while Scott is mostly silent and serious.\n\nOut searching, they have a couple of false alarms before Scott heads into a carnival and encounters \"the Blob\" which Professor X affirms is the mutant they seek.&nbsp; Scott watches as people try to move the Blob to no avail.&nbsp; Then they shoot him with bullets, but they just sink into his flesh until he flexes it, and they all fall out.&nbsp; Scott approaches him in his trailer later, but only Jean's eventual appearance convinces him to visit the X-Men headquarters.&nbsp; The Professor realizes that once the Blob sets himself, he literally cannot be moved.\n\nThe professor offers him admittance to the X-Men, but the Blob considers himself better than all of them and <i>refuses</i>!&nbsp; Professor X is incensed, and because he has learned their identities, he'll have to remove that memory from his brain!&nbsp; But the Blob evades capture (after a couple of pages of battle action) and escapes through a hatch to crawl back to the city, where he plans to re-join the carnival.\n\nThe Professor says they can't risk him knowing their secret location and identities.&nbsp; \"He must not talk!\"&nbsp; But the Blob <i>also</i> realizes that he's dangerous to the X-Men, and now that he's learned he's a mutant, he takes over the carnival with the plan to attack the X-Men first before they come after him, which he knows they will.&nbsp; The entire carnival heads out to X-Men HQ, and the prof knows he will have to wipe this memory from all of them now!\n\nThe entire carnival attacks, and though the X-Men fight valiantly, there are simply too many of them to handle.&nbsp; They are finally overwhelmed by all the actors and animals, and the Blob leads his way toward the Professor!&nbsp; However, the professor helps Jean to realize that she's able to free everyone by telekinetically summoning the knife-thrower's utensils to cut everyone's ropes, and the group heads inside in time to help the professor!\n\nThe ensuing scuffle gives the professor time to finish preparing his \"intensifier ray\" that augments his ability, and he quickly makes them all forget they've ever met the X-Men.&nbsp; They're left wondering where they are and that they better get back to the carnival before they get \"sacked\" (as the Blob puts it - is he British perhaps?&nbsp; He also uses the term \"rubes\" to describe those who gawk at him at the carnival).\n\nTheir memories wiped, the carnival folks return to their lives, and the Blob no longer even realizes that he's a mutant.&nbsp; All is well, with the ending text hinting that because the Blob is a mutant, perhaps one day his memory will return.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "<i>far</i> better than the last issue.&nbsp; The Blob is depicted as selfish and ego-centric, interested in only himself and what he can get as a \"freak\" of nature and seems almost <i>happy</i> to learn he's better than his fellow men.&nbsp; Professor X here needs a gadget to help wipe the minds of the carnival men, whereas before, in the previous issue, he takes on the Vanisher's men virtually single-handedly, though he doesn't have to mind-alter anyone <i>but</i> the Vanisher there, so I don't really consider it all a stretch that the more strenuous task here requires a bit of technological help.\n\nThe different characterizations of the members really start to take shape here.&nbsp; Hank is seen reading an \"Advanced Calculus\" book with his foot and speaking with a large vocabulary.&nbsp; Scott is silent and brooding.&nbsp; Bobby is young and brash and quick to start a scuffle (very much like the way Johnny starts brawls with Ben in the FF).&nbsp; Angel is shown as the \"glamor boy.\"&nbsp; Jean is less defined here and is spoken <i>about</i> more by the others than what we see directly from her, but she clearly prefers Scott to the others also.\n\nThe Professor is also shown as a little too quick to offer X-Men membership to newcomers.&nbsp; Let's hope this encounter will serve as a learning experience for him as well as his X-Men.&nbsp; They're constantly learning too, and this is a good thing - this is a school after all, and I kind of like that aspect of it.&nbsp; Decades later, we see the same sort of thing with Harry Potter and the school he and his fellow wizards attend.\n\nNote that Stan Lee is not known for letting his female characters save the day very often, but we see a taste of it here where Jean is the one who's able to free the others.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis book hasn't really hit its stride, but this was a step in the right direction.\n\nNote that the Announcements section of <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> #9 mentions that this issue of the X-Men \"really plays up their various personalities!\"&nbsp; People were apparently thinking that the Beast was too much like the Thing, and it's clear in this issue he absolutely is <i>not</i>!\n\nThe Announcements page of [LINK:1963:FF_23|FF #23|2027-03-16] reveals that the Blob got his name from a sandwich delivery boy who, upon seeing what Lee and Kirby were looking at from the drawing said \"it looks like a big blob\" - so they named him the Blob!",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}